<details class="drawing-guide" id="tm-help">
 <summary>Help and guide — locations, measurements, controls and saving</summary>
 <p>Use this reference whenever you need an explanation. For a guided practice session, use <a href="#training-heading">training mode</a>. Your job drawing stays separate from the practice sketch.</p>
 <nav aria-label="Drawing help topics"><a href="#tm-help-location">Location and map</a><a href="#tm-help-scope">Slip roads and site layout</a><a href="#tm-help-measure">Measurements</a><a href="#tm-help-tools">Drawing tools</a><a href="#tm-help-files">Saving and downloads</a></nav>
 <section><h4>Create an AI-assisted drawing</h4><p>Search your postcode and choose the exact site. Set the map view, including the full approaches and departures. Choose aerial imagery when it helps identify the carriageway. Press <strong>Mark work start + direction (AI)</strong>, tap the actual work start, then a second point along the correct carriageway in the direction of travel. Enter the work length and width, road type, speed, lane widths and lane to close. Describe the job and press <strong>Create AI drawing from this map</strong>.</p><p>The AI receives the selected map image and the traffic knowledge used by the work-pack system. It traces the road; the calculator places supported signs, cones, tapers and the work boundary by distance. All items remain editable. It does not automatically approve the drawing. A works-access request adds a proposed position label; its cone gap and vehicle route need manual design.</p><p>The first AI version supports the existing standard profiles: two-way signals on a two-lane single carriageway or a single outer-lane closure on a 2–4 lane dual carriageway/motorway. Interior or multiple lane closures, affected slip roads/junctions and other complex cases return review points rather than a complete equipment drawing. Keep your actual lane widths: unsupported dimensions need another profile, not altered measurements.</p><p>AI requests use the existing API account and are limited to one per minute. Your selected map, job description and dimensions are sent for analysis. A failed or review-needed response leaves the current drawing unchanged. Changing the map or inputs during analysis prevents application of a stale result.</p></section>
 <section id="tm-help-location"><h4>1. Find the location and use the map</h4>
 <p>Enter a postcode or a road and town, press <strong>Find location</strong>, then select a result. A postcode is an approximate area: check the road and move closer to the exact work location. A what3words address works only when that service is configured.</p>
 <dl><dt>Map view and Measure distance / width</dt><dd>Choose Road map or Aerial with road labels. Press Measure distance / width and tap two points to estimate the straight-line distance in metres. Copy it into a width or length field as appropriate and choose Map-measured. Follow road bends with separate measurements; a single straight measurement cuts across bends. The temporary measurement line is not part of the exported drawing.</dd><dt>Map zoom in / out (Map + / Map −)</dt><dd>Changes the geographical area shown. Zoom in to see the road layout; zoom out to include advance signs. These controls are directly above the map.</dd>
 <dt>North, South, East, West</dt><dd>Moves the map in that direction. Set the view before adding your drawing: changing the map can clear existing symbols after a confirmation, so save first.</dd>
 <dt>Mark working section</dt><dd>Press this button, then tap the start and end of the actual working section on the road centreline, in the direction of travel. Do not include the whole advance-signing area in these two points. Enter the surveyed working-space length in the measurements.</dd>
 <dt>Mark junction / access</dt><dd>Press this button, then tap the feature on the map. It records a point for review and blocks the simple automatic generator. It does not calculate a junction design.</dd>
 <dt>Remove map / start fresh</dt><dd>Removes the map and its associated drawing after any required confirmation. Save a project first if you need to keep it.</dd>
 <dt>Drawing zoom and Pan view</dt><dd>Fit view shows the whole drawing; 150%, 200% and 300% enlarge it without changing the map area or real distances. Turn on Pan view to scroll the enlarged drawing on a phone; return to editing to place items.</dd></dl>
 <p>A map is background context, not a lane survey. It may be outdated and does not automatically identify every junction, access or road hazard. <a href="#tm-map-controls">Go to the map controls ↓</a></p></section>
 <section id="tm-help-scope"><h4>2. Choose the site layout</h4>
 <p><strong>Simple straight road</strong> is for supported cases without affected junctions or the other excluded features listed in the confirmation. Check the full area, including advance signs and traffic queues.</p>
 <p><strong>Slip road / junction / roundabout / affected access</strong> means the simple automatic generator is unsuitable. Use the map and manual drawing tools for a competent designer to develop the layout. A lane-one closure with an affected slip road belongs here. Leave the straight-road confirmation unticked.</p>
 <p>The confirmation is a statement about the real site, not a button to unlock the app. Training mode does not bypass it. Level crossings, guide islands, contraflow, multiple closed lanes and mobile working also need a separate design.</p></section>
 <section id="tm-help-measure"><h4>3. Enter road and working-space measurements</h4><dl>
 <dt>Road name</dt><dd>The road or site name printed in the draft and report.</dd>
 <dt>Road / layout</dt><dd>Choose single carriageway for the supported two-way portable-signal layout, or dual carriageway/motorway for a supported one-lane static closure. This choice does not establish that the site is suitable.</dd>
 <dt>Permanent speed limit</dt><dd>The existing permanent limit, in mph, used by the calculator. It is not a proposed temporary speed restriction.</dd>
 <dt>Lanes on the modelled carriageway</dt><dd>The number of lanes on the carriageway being modelled. On a dual carriageway, do not add the lanes travelling the other way.</dd>
 <dt>Closed side in increasing-chainage direction</dt><dd>Which side is closed when looking in the direction in which your distances increase: nearside / left or offside / right. Check it against the actual direction of travel.</dd>
 <dt>Lane widths and width source</dt><dd>Enter each lane’s width in metres. The initial 3 m is an assumption; choose Surveyed only when you have entered measured widths. Total carriageway width adds those lane widths. The automatic profiles have their own permitted ranges and may reject a site: do not alter real measurements to make it pass.</dd>
 <dt>Working-space width</dt><dd>The width needed for the work, including plant and materials, excluding the separately calculated traffic-facing safety clearance.</dd>
 <dt>Working-space length</dt><dd>The surveyed length of the actual work area, in metres. This is not the total length including tapers and advance signs.</dd>
 <dt>Start of working space — chainage</dt><dd>Distance along the road from your chosen reference point to the start of work. If work starts at that point, enter 0; if it starts 50 m further along, enter 50. Chainage is a distance, not a postcode or map coordinate.</dd>
 <dt>Hard shoulder present</dt><dd>Records the shoulder. A nearside closure involving it needs a separate design.</dd>
 <dt>Congestion likely</dt><dd>Requests the calculator’s extended dual-carriageway advance signing. It does not predict queues or assess junction effects.</dd></dl></section>
 <section><h4>4. Calculate and read the result</h4><p>Load a map, mark the working section and enter the site details before pressing <strong>Calculate and draw editable draft</strong>. Read the status message if generation is blocked. Common causes include missing points, unsupported lane dimensions, an affected junction, a mismatch between the marked and measured length, or signs falling outside the map view.</p>
 <p>Open <strong>Calculated distances, sign schedule and cone quantities</strong> to review the report. The cone quantity covers only the stated core runs, not all spare or site-specific cones. Provisional sign positions need checking. Signal timings and design approval are not calculated.</p></section>
 <section id="tm-help-tools"><h4>5. Edit the drawing</h4><dl>
 <dt>Road, barrier and works area</dt><dd>Choose the tool, then drag between the start and end positions.</dd>
 <dt>Cone, sign, traffic lights and label</dt><dd>Choose a tool, then tap the drawing to place it. For signs choose a Sign face; use Label / sign text for the wording.</dd>
 <dt>Select / move</dt><dd>Choose this tool, select an item and drag it to reposition it. Select an item to edit its text. Manual changes invalidate the calculated schedule; the changed sketch needs review.</dd>
 <dt>Undo / Delete selected / New drawing</dt><dd>Undo reverses the previous drawing edit. Delete selected removes the selected item. New drawing clears the current drawing after confirmation; export it first if needed.</dd>
 <dt>Place items using coordinates</dt><dd>A keyboard alternative: choose the tool, enter X and Y (plus End X and End Y for lines/areas), then press Add item. These are sketch coordinates, not metres or surveyed map coordinates.</dd></dl>
 <p>Symbols are illustrative and enlarged for readability. A leader line and dot indicate a symbol’s original anchor when its face has been displaced to avoid overlap. A grid is a drawing guide, not a distance scale.</p></section>
 <section id="tm-help-files"><h4>6. Save, reopen and upload files</h4><dl>
 <dt>Download drawing PDF</dt><dd>Downloads a viewable drawing and any included report. Keep map attribution visible. A PDF does not approve the design.</dd>
 <dt>Export DXF (outlines)</dt><dd>Downloads vector outlines for compatible CAD software. The map image is not included, and sketch units are not surveyed CAD coordinates.</dd>
 <dt>Save editable data (.json)</dt><dd>Saves the editable project. JSON looks like code when opened directly; use Open project to load it back into this app. Map image bytes are not saved; a saved map needs to be fetched again and old location metadata can expire.</dd>
 <dt>Open project</dt><dd>Choose a previously saved project JSON file to restore it. Save the current drawing first if you need to keep both.</dd>
 <dt>Autosave</dt><dd>Job drawings save only in this browser on this device, where storage is available. They are not saved to your account or attached to a paid work pack. Clearing browser data can erase them.</dd>
 <dt>Upload an existing drawing</dt><dd>Choose a file up to 20 MB. Images and PDFs can be previewed; DXF preview supports only basic entities. DWG needs a compatible CAD viewer or an export to PDF/image/DXF. Uploaded originals remain in the tab’s memory and are removed when it closes or reloads. Uploading does not attach a file to checkout.</dd></dl></section>
 <p><strong>Before site use:</strong> a competent traffic-management designer must check the real site, visibility, accesses, swept paths, queues, pedestrians, signs and authority requirements. This guide explains the app controls.</p>
</details>
